 combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271316bb-de9b-5d8b-bad0-ffd1bfc5b101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420447} {:answer/id #uuid "633d97e3-8d84-57c9-8ac7-bdd2378d9d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420449} {:answer/id #uuid "c2051bdd-22cb-509c-8fb6-340e90eb858d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420456} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e4325a-2b33-5c98-a020-248e21f996f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420459} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d19e05-07c5-5adc-9a1c-131ee586eacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "271316bb-de9b-5d8b-bad0-ffd1bfc5b101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420447} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f94aaa-a8cf-5aa4-9f93-884e35c10a15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420464}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b667d36f-710b-5326-b636-9597ec1ec3f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-77230" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cf7d0520b1d0654d5749651595acd48b7a08f2b2d55142b18ca63b2ded20f55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9e/9edea1e3ad727d11d7bfac884f27c1b435233fa7df561dbe2a4f3af45ea16bfa.png)\n\nIn $△OBC$, we have:\n\n- $BC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=3a.$ \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =2b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=2b+\\lambda a=2b+3a.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096447 :question/id #uuid "2a61b115-4e26-5966-b2a9-d2fedef6b728" :question/math-academy-id "e-4282" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $3$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a66a99-6b4a-5ad5-8922-6f48006ff183" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048882 :topic/math-academy-id 1101 :topic/title "The Magnitude of a Vector"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049715 :topic/math-academy-id 1103 :topic/title "Parallel Vectors"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be1529be-a7de-5974-b991-d60dde835856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093789} {:answer/id #uuid "d540dace-2707-5500-b5c4-ab774e31fbd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093790} {:answer/id #uuid "03b0e61c-7ea0-5634-9db6-b0e0196b04fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093791} {:answer/id #uuid "40cede01-8d57-5e23-8ad3-1718dcad0ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :a